# Summaries from Hmisc: hmisc

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`mean_cl_boot()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/hmisc.html), which covers
`mean_cl_normal()`, `mean_sdl()` and `median_hilow()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each
example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its
ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

These functions wrap summary functions from the Hmisc package so that `stat_summary()` can use
them: each takes a numeric vector and returns a data frame of one row, a central value in `y` and
the ends of an interval around it in `ymin` and `ymax`. `mean_cl_boot()`, `mean_cl_normal()` and
`mean_sdl()` centre on the mean, and `median_hilow()` on the median. Not in Giggly yet: these
four functions, and `stat_summary()`.

## Summaries of random values

```r
if (requireNamespace("Hmisc", quietly = TRUE)) {
set.seed(1)
x <- rnorm(100)
mean_cl_boot(x)
mean_cl_normal(x)
mean_sdl(x)
median_hilow(x)
}
```

The example runs only where Hmisc is installed. With `set.seed(1)` fixing R's random numbers,
`rnorm(100)` draws 100 values from a standard normal distribution, and each function returns its
summary of them. Not in Giggly yet: `mean_cl_boot()`, `mean_cl_normal()`, `mean_sdl()` and
`median_hilow()`.
